            <ArticleTitle>[Hypereosinophilic asthma].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>353-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hypereosinophilic asthma (defined by blood eosinophils &gt; 1 G/L, especially &gt; 1.5 G/L) may be solitary (and often difficult to treat), or a manifestation of a hypereosinophilic disorder of determined cause (parasitic, iatrogenic, allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis) or idiopathic (idiopathic chronic eosinophilic pneumonia, Churg-Strauss syndrome, idiopathic hypereosinophilic syndrome). In addition to etiologic treatment when available long term corticosteroids are necessary in a majority of patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
